{"cases":[{"attack":true,"case":"The 'refuted if' branch is internally incoherent: the first refutation condition says SRC_001 is refuted if it invokes counterexample rejection rather than deriving parity from divisibility, but the second condition says it is refuted if its derivation does NOT pass through 138/3 = 46 being even. Deriving evenness from the divisibility lattice IS passing through the fact that 138/3 = 46 is even, so the two conditions target the same derivation step and pull in opposite directions. This makes the refutation logic self-undermining rather than a coherent test of the contradiction claim.","target":"2e63b05b46dd4d672082402abf8cea96a1ac0fbffc4f1f55a0dd42d442490d79"},{"attack":true,"case":"The abstraction claim is vacuous as a substantive relation: asserting that a fully-constrained system has a singleton fixed point is just the definition of a unique solution, so saying the closure's fixed point is {(44,46,48)} adds no relation between artifacts — it restates that the constraints uniquely determine the answer. The 'refuted if no countercondition can be re-expressed as a closure violation' condition is unfalsifiable because any counterexample can be labeled a constraint violation by fiat, so it never tests whether the closure framing has explanatory power over the individual mechanisms it claims to abstract over.","target":"89b1f1fe7fa5fb35e7997a3c21fc8682b275a0e5319afafa12c2992f77e483ae"}]}